<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    A class library, never the web SDK: this layer persists and secures, it does
    not host HTTP. So no ASP.NET Core package and no framework reference to the
    ASP.NET Core shared framework may be added here.

    The reference on Application is deliberate rather than incidental: some of
    the abstractions this layer implements are declared there, and its options
    classes are bound here through IOptions<T>. Never reference DnnMigration.Api,
    which already references this project and would invert the graph, and never
    reference a test project or a legacy project under Library/ or Website/.

    This project owns every Entity Framework Core, SQL client, password-hashing,
    health-check and token-signing reference in the solution, and nothing else.
    Swagger, versioning and logging belong to Api; validation belongs to
    Application; the test database providers belong to the integration test
    project, because shipping one here would put a test provider into production.

    JWT is deliberately split across two projects rather than owned by one,
    because minting and validating a token are different jobs living in different
    layers. This project mints — JwtTokenService signs the access token — so it
    references the signing library. Api validates every inbound token on the
    request path, which is middleware, so it references the ASP.NET Core bearer
    authentication handler. Moving either reference to the other project would
    force one of those two responsibilities into a layer that must not hold it.

    Build policy is inherited from backend/Directory.Build.props, which is why
    this file declares no PropertyGroup. Restating or overriding an inherited
    property would weaken the Release build's warnings-as-errors policy.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>

    <!--
      The 8.0.29 band matches the installed shared framework exactly. Every
      version here is exact-pinned: no floating range, wildcard or moving tag.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      Provider for the existing, unaltered SQL Server database. The legacy
      provider registration uses an empty object qualifier and "dbo" as the
      database owner, so those are the defaults entity configurations bind to.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      Keep this reference PLAIN — adding PrivateAssets="all", the shape the stock
      template uses, stops the design-time library flowing to referencing
      projects. The EF CLI resolves design-time services from the startup
      project, and the API project receives this package only transitively from
      here, so severing the flow breaks migration tooling with the familiar
      "startup project does not reference Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design"
      error. The separate command-line tooling package is not referenced;
      dotnet-ef is installed as a global tool.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      EXPLICIT DIRECT PIN AT THE VERSION THE FROZEN DEPENDENCY INVENTORY NAMES.
      AAP section 0.6.1 pins Microsoft.Data.SqlClient at 5.2.3 and says why the
      pin is direct rather than transitive: without it the SQL Server provider
      resolves 5.1.7 through EF Core, and the pin was verified to change the
      resolved graph.

      A previous revision raised this to 6.1.6 on the argument that the 5.x
      identity chain carries deprecated authentication and JSON packages. That
      argument is not this project's to make. The inventory is FROZEN - it is the
      agreed dependency graph, verified by a clean restore and a Release build
      with warnings promoted to errors - and a project that raises a pinned major
      version on its own is no longer building the graph that was reviewed.
      Raising it is a two-step, review-visible operation: change the inventory,
      then change this line. Restoring it here also restores the exact resolved
      graph the lock files below assert, and the restore was re-verified to
      produce zero warnings, so no advisory is promoted to an error by the
      warnings-as-errors policy.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="5.2.3" />

    <!--
      One-way password hashing, replacing a reversibly-encrypted credential store
      whose key was committed to the legacy configuration. The migration-only
      LegacyCredentialVerifier performs bounded, opt-in comparison of legacy rows;
      AuthService immediately replaces an accepted value through this package's
      one-way BCrypt implementation, with administrative reset as the fallback.
      Security/BcryptPasswordHasher.cs uses this package's ENHANCED hash and
      verify pair, which pre-digests the credential and so removes the plain
      algorithm's 72-byte input-equivalence behaviour; the two halves must always
      be changed together. The legacy password policy is preserved rather than
      tightened mid-migration. Both divergences are recorded in
      MIGRATION_NOTES.md.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="BCrypt.Net-Next" Version="4.0.3" />

    <!--
      The health-check ABSTRACTIONS, which is why this reference is load-bearing
      even though its own SQL Server check is no longer registered. This project
      is a plain class library rather than a web project, so it has no framework
      reference to draw Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks from, and
      this package is the SOLE route by which IHealthCheck, HealthCheckResult,
      HealthStatus, AddHealthChecks() and AddCheck<T>() reach
      HealthChecks/DatabaseHealthCheck.cs and DependencyInjection.cs - verified
      against the restored dependency graph, not assumed. Removing it does not
      remove a dead dependency; it breaks the compile.

      AddSqlServer() is deliberately NOT called. It reads the same
      ConnectionStrings:Default value and opens a connection to the same instance
      as this project's own probe, so registering both put two connections behind
      every request to a path that docker-compose, the Alpine image's wget
      HEALTHCHECK and any orchestrator poll continuously, while distinguishing
      nothing. The package is retained for the abstractions and remains pinned per
      AAP section 0.6.1; the endpoint it serves must stay anonymous and reachable,
      because docker-compose gates the frontend service on the API becoming
      healthy.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AspNetCore.HealthChecks.SqlServer" Version="8.0.2" />

    <!--
      ACCESS-TOKEN SIGNING FOR JwtTokenService, THROUGH THE ONE JWT IDENTITY THE
      FROZEN INVENTORY NAMES. AAP section 0.4.1.1 assigns "EF Core, BCrypt, JWT
      packages" to this project, and section 0.6.1 lists exactly one JWT package:
      Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer 8.0.29. This is that package.

      A previous revision instead declared System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt 7.7.3
      directly - an identity the inventory does not contain - on the argument that
      a class library should reference the token library alone. The version
      reasoning in that argument was sound and is preserved here, because it is
      exactly what this reference delivers: JwtBearer 8.0.29 resolves
      Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect 7.7.3, which brings
      System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt 7.7.3 and Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
      7.7.3 - the same band the Api project's own JwtBearer reference resolves. So
      the assembly that signs a token here and the assembly that validates it on
      the request path are one loaded version, and a signature that verifies in a
      unit test verifies in production. The alternative - letting the token library
      arrive as an untracked transitive of the data provider - would compile
      against the 6.35.0 band while running against 7.7.3, which is precisely the
      split the earlier note set out to prevent.

      The cost is stated rather than glossed: this reference brings a framework
      reference to Microsoft.AspNetCore.App with it. Nothing in this project
      touches the request pipeline and nothing may start doing so - authentication
      handlers, middleware and endpoint plumbing belong to the Api layer, and the
      Domain project remains free of every package. Bump this and the Api's
      JwtBearer reference together or neither.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="8.0.29" />

  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Domain\DnnMigration.Domain.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Application\DnnMigration.Application.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ONE FRIEND ASSEMBLY, NAMED EXPLICITLY, AND NOTHING WIDENED TO PUBLIC FOR A TEST.

    Everything this layer implements is reachable through a PUBLIC contract - IPasswordHasher,
    IClock, ICacheService, IHostSettingsService, IPortalContext and IRefreshTokenStore from
    Domain, ITokenService and IPermissionEvaluator from Application, the nine repository
    interfaces and IUnitOfWork from Domain - and every one of them is registered by
    AddInfrastructure. That is the PREFERRED route, and it is the one the integration suites take:
    DnnMigration.IntegrationTests/Security/ resolves the real password hasher, token service,
    permission evaluator and refresh-token store from the composed container, which proves the
    registration as well as the behaviour. Nothing here should be reached by `new` when the
    container can hand it over.

    The grant below exists for the components whose behaviour is asserted WITHOUT a host, from
    DnnMigration.UnitTests: LoggingAuditSink, LegacyCredentialVerifier, JwtTokenService and
    DatabaseHealthCheck are each internal sealed and each is covered by a suite that substitutes
    its collaborators and asserts what it does with them - the audit sink's structured-property
    discipline, the legacy verifier's format handling, the token service's claim composition and
    the health check's non-disclosure of connection detail. Those facts need no database, no
    Kestrel and no policy pipeline, so a host would only slow them down; what they do need is the
    type. AAP 0.5.1.5 places exactly these Security suites in the unit project, which is why the
    edge and this grant are both declared rather than worked around.

    The production layering guarantee is untouched and is a property of the four PRODUCTION
    project files, not of this item: Domain declares zero project references, Application cannot
    see Infrastructure, and either violation remains a compile error. A test project is the
    outermost consumer of the graph rather than a layer within it.

    The grant names ONE assembly. Do not add a second, do not grant it to a wildcard, and do not
    widen a type from internal to public to make a test compile - including DnnDbContext, whose
    internal declaration is what keeps Application away from the model.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DnnMigration.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
